#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""autofinish-run — a fresh, unattended `/finish` on one claude/* worktree, and how it ended.

    autofinish-run WORKTREE [--rule A|B|C|D|E|L|M] [--by sweep|override|brief|typed|yolo|dnd|chain|ship|handed] [--merge | --hold]
                   [--digest D] [--session ID] [--on here] [--priority high|force] [--dry]
    autofinish-run notify [WORKTREE]      the run's own session: whom to message, and the text (below)
    autofinish-run queued QID --repo R --branch B   a claimed finish's start: its launchd job's verb (Where, below)
    autofinish-run waiter                 detach()'s exec'd grandchild, its spec in AUTOFINISH_WAITER_SPEC; never typed

Where (finish-as-task, 2026-11-02; the owner's D3 of docs/node-lands-2026-10-31.md: "The finish should be handled
like any other task … finishes assigned to the queue should be picked up similarly to other non-finish tasks"): on a
host with a queue home, every finish is one fleet entry of kind finish (dispatch-queue's header, Finishes). This
process, on the host that asks, runs the refusals below under its start lock, the origin gate and the landing
ladder, ends a warm run of the branch here (its last checkpoint is the tip), pushes a tip origin lacks (never
forced), and puts the entry through `dispatch --queue-finish` (dispatch's own host facts): the tip, and the rights
the run will have — by, rule, merge, hold, from (this host, whose yes it is: /finish step 11.4 takes its path) and a
ship's digest. Claimed here at once when this host has room; else any host with room claims it, this one first for a
grace it gives up the moment its claim finds no room (the queue's --prefer). Pinned here instead when only this host
can judge or run it: `--on here`; the landing ladder (a hold, a mode, a mode or ship launch, `--by brief|handed`,
rule M — landing() reads this host's plans; ladder_stay); a tip origin lacks after a push that failed, or a worktree
holding what origin lacks (the queue's held_here at the put). Nothing of the run is written here: no state, no lock,
no waiter. It prints `queued: QID (a finish of <tip>), …` with the window's name and exits 0 (every caller reads 0
as the finish in hand, a live finish of the branch (`busy … (a finish)`) included); a put the queue refuses — a
tombstone of this tip (`ran …`: one run per tip), a build step queued — is a refusal, exit 3; a put the queue alone could not take
(its exit 1, no JSON: no home after all, a home that refused the write) starts here, as before the queue.
The claim's job (dispatch-queue start_job: `dispatch BRANCH --repo R --finish --from-queue QID`, which execs
`autofinish-run queued QID`) reads the entry under its own claim (`mine --json`, refused when the entry does not
read), finds the branch's worktree here or makes it from origin at the entry's tip (queued_worktree, as `pickup
open` does), and starts the run as below with those rights: `--from=<that host>` in the prompt when another host put
it, `--merge` only where the entry carries a person's yes. Its dispatch --start holds the claim (`--queued QID --job
L`: the claim's slot, `mine` asked again under the start lock, `started` with the session) and its end leaves the
entry's tombstone (`ended`), to which record() adds the outcome (`finished`; a start that never ran takes it away).
What keeps the claimed start from running goes back: `refused` (queued again, this host skipping it the hour) for
this host's own state, `failed` (its tombstone holding the tip) for a reason any host would meet — origin's branch
no longer at the tip, the same tip already run here. No host waits on, watches or holds a lock for a run another
host runs: the queue is the one record. Without a queue home, the run starts here (below), as before the queue.
The bundle is this host's: a finish claimed from another host's put says at its head that the branch's sessions ran
there (no transcript rides in an entry). The peer road this replaced (`wharf-runs/finish-on`, orca-pairs' landing
peer, peer-start, peer-sync, its tickets and waiter) is gone; so is the Mac-local landing queue.

E1 of docs/autofinish-design-2026-10-02.md (D13): the runner. Whoever decides that a branch should
launch without the owner's wait — the sweep (rules A, B, D, E, L), `/autofinish` (C), a typed `/finish` handed
off cold (E7), the end of a `dispatch --yolo` or dnd build run (E12, `--by yolo|dnd`: the prompt becomes
`/finish <branch> --unattended --yolo|--dnd`) — calls this, and it returns once dispatch has said what it did (below).

The landing ladder (skipper-modes S3b; the owner, 2026-10-21: "dnd: I want to automerge as well"): before
the start, landing() reads the branch's mode from plan.json itself (bin/autofinish's landing_mode) and
decides whether this run may merge. `ask`: every launch but a person's stops at a ready PR. A mode
launch (`--by yolo|dnd`, or the sweep's rule M): `yolo` merges on any host bar the launcher's gates and
the repo's `autofinish_exclude` and `in_place`, `dnd` bar the held slice (bin/autofinish's held_slice:
money, a physical repo, a public publish, a deleted data file, `autofinish_exclude`), and a mode
lowered since holds — but a yolo launch with no mode at all in a `"merge": "auto"` repo, from a caller
without CLAUDE_UNATTENDED, keeps that standing yes (E12). A hand-over (`--by handed`: dispatch landing a
done `default` build; task-shape S2) is judged as a typed /finish under that design's D1-C — no slice holds
it (bin/autofinish's handed_held, an empty HANDED_SLICE), bar a diff or config that will not read, a binary and
the launcher's own gates, a `.gitattributes` change and a mode that will not read — yet it is no person's yes: mode
ask holds it, `--merge` is refused, rule M makes it a
mode launch, and its finish is pinned to the host that put it (ladder_stay); a chain relaunch keeps it. A hold adds `--hold` to the prompt, denies
`bin/pr merge` to the run, writes `"hold": "<why>"` into the state, and a stop at step 11 is recorded
with gate `held` and the class (`stopped <branch> held held (<why>): …`), which helm's board cards.
`--by` itself grants nothing: the yes to merge is the mode, which only the owner raises
(helmlib.raise_refusal), so an unattended caller typing `--by yolo` gets what plan.json says. Defaults: --rule C, --by override (a person
calling it by hand is closest to `/autofinish`). --merge: the owner's `--merge` (/finish's: their explicit
yes to merge this branch's PR, given up front), carried into the run: the prompt becomes
`/finish <branch> --unattended --merge`, and on the build Mac step 11.4 then merges without asking. It
covers that one branch's PR only, and only a person gives it: `--by override` or `--by typed` (cold_prompt
passes `--by typed --merge` on the laptop, where a typed /finish is that yes, so a finish another host claims
carries it into that host's prompt with `--from`; the night-landings digest's D3-A, 2026-10-25); with
`--by sweep`, `chain` or `yolo` it is refused at once (exit 2) — the sweep and the chain have no
person's yes, and a yolo launch outside a `"merge": "auto"` repo stops at a held PR on purpose (E12).
So is any caller with CLAUDE_UNATTENDED set (exit 2): every dispatched run has it, and its allow rules
pass `bin/*` unseen, so a run the sweep started could otherwise launch another that merges. So is
AUTOFINISH_CALLER_UNATTENDED (a claimed finish whose put came from an unattended caller: queued_main sets it). The putting host's refusal is the one that
counts: the claim's job carries the entry's yes, judged at its put (finish_bad() refuses a merge no person gave). The label
is all that is checked otherwise, the caller's word that a person said yes. A chain relaunch of a
`--merge` run does not carry it, nor a sweep retry of one that died: either stops at the PR on the
claiming host, while a retry from the laptop merges by the laptop's path (`--from` wherever it is claimed). --session: the caller's own session (E7's hook
passes the panel's), never a lock holder it is refused for. A skipper (SKIPPER_SCOPE set) is refused
--merge as an unattended run is: its yes is the keel's, carried as `--by ship`, never its own word.

A ship's launch (skipper-resilience S6; the yard component's "The merge default"): the yard's tick
(`wharf apply --once --all` on a scope's yard, bin/wharf's start_launch) launches a ship's `launch:
finish` block with `--merge --by ship --digest D` when the keel said merge, else `--hold`. `--by ship`
stands for a person's yes given at the keel — the owner's attended `wharf apply --yard`, whose blueprint
said `merge: yes` or nothing — so this re-reads it rather than trusting the label: D must be the pin
of exactly one un-ended ship on this Mac (plans/<id>/plan.json, helm's helmlib), its pinned copy
(plans/<id>/blueprint-<ship>.md, the committed text the yard read from origin) must be at D and not
say `merge: no`, the plan must record `laid_by.mode: attended` and no keel `merge: no`, and the
worktree must be in the pin's repo, on a block branch of that ship (a branch name alone repeats
across repos). `--by ship --hold` is the tick's launch where the keel said no: the same caller
refusal, no digest asked, nothing merged. The caller must be the tick, not a session: it
is refused under CLAUDE_UNATTENDED, SKIPPER_SCOPE, CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, a DISPATCH_DEPTH past 0 or
a ship's own env (DISPATCH_SHIP, DISPATCH_PLAN: a block's command), exit 2 — launchd's tick has none
of them, and every dispatched run, skipper and block has one (the checkpoint hook denies a run that
strips CLAUDE_UNATTENDED). The mode still holds a ship launch in `ask`: `ship` is no person's label to
landing(). --hold: the caller holds the landing at a ready PR (the keel's no), whatever the mode —
the prompt gains `--hold` (finish_prompt), `bin/pr merge` stays denied, and the state's `hold` says
`the keel holds it (--hold)`; with --merge it is refused, the two ask opposite things. That hold is
sticky: any later launch of the worktree (a sweep retry of a run that died, a chain) keeps it, since
only the keel could lift it. A launch the ladder holds carries no `--merge` into its prompt.

--priority high|force (force-any; the owner, 2026-10-30: "force should also increase priority of a finish
if needed. let's not discriminate on the kind of task"): the finish's rank in the queue, as dispatch's
--priority is a build's — force one slot over the cap and on the force's memory floor; a finish ranks ahead of
a build of the same priority (dispatch-queue's rank()). Put in the entry, and passed to `dispatch --start
--priority` by the claim's start. Judged here, in this process: dispatch --start runs under the detached waiter, which drops the
caller's session (its env and its parent), so dispatch's own force-ok would see no session there. force:
never an unattended caller (REGATTA_UNATTENDED, CLAUDE_UNATTENDED, SKIPPER_SCOPE, AUTOFINISH_CALLER_UNATTENDED), else `dispatch-queue force-ok` (the owner's terminal, or a session
holding its force mark), the mark spent once the run started or was queued (exit 0); high from an
unattended caller only when its own run's record is high (caller-prio). A finish of this branch already
queued is raised in place (`dispatch-queue raise`), never started twice: the call prints raise's line and
exits 0. Neither a chain relaunch nor a sweep retry carries it: force
buys one run.

A ship's lease (skipper-resilience S4): when DISPATCH_SHIP (or the older DISPATCH_PLAN) names a ship
that has one here (plans/<id>/skipper.json, helm's bin/leaselib.py), the caller's generation is
checked first — SKIPPER_GEN, else the lease's own session, else a plain shell — and any other is
refused, exit 4, with one event in the ship (none on --dry). No lease, no DISPATCH_SHIP, or helm not
installed: nothing is checked, as before.

Before the start, in this process, under `<git dir>/claude/autofinish.json.lock` (O_EXCL; one
older than START_LOCK_S is a start that died, taken over), so two callers never both pass the
refusals and both write:
  - the origin gate (tip_gate(), finish-stale-tip, 2026-11-01: a run started on a seed whose build sat five commits
    ahead on origin, and it "stayed on this Mac" and went on), on both roads, here and sent, after what no tree moves
    under (tree_refusal(): a run still going, a run launched on this tip, whose own push may have moved origin; then
    a live lock) and before the refusals that read the tip, so they judge the tip that lands. Origin's branch is
    read (ls-remote, ORIGIN_READ_S); a tip equal to it or ahead of it, or no such branch, is today's start, and no
    line. Origin holding what the tip lacks refuses with ORIGIN_RC (6), nothing started, unless the tip moves:
      - the tip a strict ancestor of it: the branch's warm run is ended first (end_warm: its busy check is the one
        that sees a run on any login, and its last checkpoint may be the dirt), then a clean worktree is
        fast-forwarded (`merge --ff-only`), one line, and that tip is the one landed, its commits named at the head of
        the run's bundle (origin_note(): once the tree holds them /finish's own "take in what GitHub has" finds
        nothing, and one may be the owner's answer to a memo). Only a person's launch goes on (judged_stop(): PERSON_BY,
        and none of PRIO_UNATTENDED): every other caller's gates judged the old tip, so it moves the tree and stops,
        and the next start judges the new one (nothing ran). `--by override` is a label: the sweep's rule C passes it
        too, on the owner's marker, and a push in the seconds since its verdict lands with that yes. A chain relaunch
        is judged on its stop's own tip first (tree_refusal()), so one main refused moves nothing;
      - the same with a dirty worktree: refused;
      - neither holding the other: refused; /finish would replay this tip onto origin's, unattended;
      - a claimed finish's start: refused, never moved — it lands the tip its entry names, and origin past it fails
        the entry for good (its tombstone holds that tip; the next tip is judged afresh).
    A commit not here is fetched first, and a fetch that fails is a refusal (the tip surely lacks it); --dry
    fetches nothing, says so and judges origin's tip. Origin that cannot be read in time is the one pass, with a line:
    a run started offline stays a start, as it was, and /finish's own step ("take in what GitHub has") meets the
    same origin. It passes only a first read: strict, past the budget, and refusing what it cannot read, are the
    reads after the tip was proved behind (once the warm run ended) and once start_here ended a run (those words
    may be that origin moved). None of these moves the tip. A tree write is never killed: the
    fast-forward is refused while there is no time for it, and has its own long timeout;
  - no retry on the same tip: a worktree whose last run (its autofinish.json) was on this very
    commit is refused, whatever that run's outcome — a stop repeats itself on the same tip, and
    a run still going is not started twice. One exception: a run dispatch refused before any
    session existed (the run cap, its start lock: gate `refused`, `not-launched` before 2026-10-17 and
    read both ways) never ran, so it may start.
    `--by chain` (the tick, orchestration B3) is judged by chain_refusal() instead:
    it relaunches only a tip whose last run stopped at `no-landing` with the network, sleep or a login's
    usage limit as the branch's last autofinished line (S4), at most CHAIN_RELAUNCHES times per tip (counted in
    `<git dir>/claude/autofinish-chain.json`), and a yolo launch it relaunches stays a yolo one;
  - the login (work-account E5, as `dispatch --account auto`; joined early on the owner's word,
    2026-10-08): `bin/account pick --repo <primary> --json` at start time, so a second login, first in
    the walk, takes the run unless the repo is on its deny list; the pick's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR goes to
    the start (unset for the default dir, whose env must stay empty — bin/account's header), and
    `account` in the state and the log line names the row. Exit 3 from the pick (no row may run
    now) is a refusal with each row's reason; exit 4 (no registry) or an unreadable answer keeps
    the login this was started with, as before. A caller's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR or
    CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN is an explicit choice and is kept, no pick, unless that dir's row has
    a window locked or is parked (`limits --all --parked --json --cached`: parked, locked, or cooling
    down after an exhaustion):
    then the pick decides, and with no row to move to it refuses (2026-10-22). A finish put in the queue keeps
    neither: the claim's job takes its own host's pick (Where). Kept:
    a panel session on the work login handing its /finish off cold, or a test (bin/autofinish's start_runner and dispatch --yolo's
    launch pass neither, so the sweep, the override and a yolo launch always pick). A pick past PICK_TIMEOUT (a probe hanging) falls back
    to `pick --cached`; --by typed picks `--cached` from the start (cold_prompt's 45 s, a hook's);
  - no lock taken from a session at work: owner.json naming a session that is not the caller,
    live in the registry (~/.claude/sessions, its pid alive) and not `idle` — busy, waiting on a
    dialog — is a refusal, whoever calls (review, 2026-10-05: a typed hand-off took one), unless
    the lock is marked `handover` (bin/pickup next; bin/wharf when it settles an Orca block, since
    2026-10-19: every session starts `sessionpipe wait` at SessionStart, so a finished builder
    reads `shell`, which counts as busy, and four launches were refused for it). A typed
    hand-off (--by typed) is refused, too, by any lock another session holds unexpired
    (checkpoint.py's held_by_other: seen under OWNER_TTL, not handed over): the owner typed /finish in
    one panel, and the branch is another's;
  - a session id is chosen, and `<git dir>/claude/autofinish.json` written with it (the contract
    the hooks read — `{"started", "session", "tip", "by", "rule", "window", "account", "ended",
    "outcome", "pr", "gate", "pushed", "base", "base_tip"}` (the last three at the end of a run a gate let
    start, task-shape S1), plus `"merge": true` for a `--merge` launch, `"hold": "<why>"` for a
    held one (S3b), `"from": "<host>"` for a finish another host put and `"queue": "<qid>"` for a claimed one
    (Where); `"host"` in a state from before the queue — each key absent otherwise — and nothing else), then the worktree lock `owner.json` (hooks/common.py's
    write_owner) in that id's name: the session holds the lock from its first call (P8), and a
    panel session still open on the branch is refused its next write, with the reason.
    Should the detach fail after that, the lock is given back (the previous owner.json, or none)
    and the state ended `never-started` at gate `refused`, exit 1: nothing ran.
Then a detached child (a double fork and setsid: the caller's process group, a hook's or a
Bash call's, may be ended the moment the caller returns — dispatch's header, 2026-10-05), which
execs this script afresh (`autofinish-run waiter`): a forked image that had resolved a name dies at its
next getaddrinfo on macOS (waiter-fork-crash, 2026-11-03; detach()'s docstring):
  - the bundle — bin/pickup's context_bundle, the branch's prompts, its last asks and replies, its
    PR — at `<git dir>/claude/autofinish-bundle.md`: the conversation's record for /finish's
    learnings and open-items gates (design section 7). Written here, not before returning: it
    reads every transcript of the repo, seconds the caller should not wait;
  - `dispatch --start "/finish <branch> --unattended[ --yolo| --dnd][ --hold][ --merge]" <worktree> "finish · <topic>"`: Remote
    Control "finish-<topic> · laptop" (the session's name, dispatch's session_label), a window tiled on Desktop 5 (bin/tiling), ended by dispatch-rc's watcher
    10 min after it goes idle — dispatch writes that `idle` into the run's record for every
    --start run, because this waiter blocks on the watcher and nobody picks a launch up again; a
    seeded branch run is kept the prompt cache's hour instead (2026-10-06), and is ended before
    this start (`dispatch-rc end-warm`; a busy one stops it — D2 of
    docs/dispatch-warm-hour-2026-10-08.md); opus (dispatch's default; a model flag only for the finisher
    rotation's arm, below); `--permission-mode auto` with dispatch's allowlist
    plus what /finish runs (Agent, EnterWorktree, git, the contract's `check:` as written on the
    primary's main, the modules' bin/* and libexec/* in the regatta main checkout, carried.py), and dispatch's denied list minus
    `git push` and bin/pr's launch verbs: checkpoint.py and the repo's pre-push judge those by
    ref. No `--background`: dispatch's default holds — background tasks off
    (CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1), so a Bash call past BASH_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS (30 min)
    is killed, not backgrounded; /finish starts its check as `nohup bin/check … &` inside one call and polls
    its summary (skills/finish step 1; bin/check reads main's check line when CLAUDE_UNATTENDED is set). Every CLAUDE* variable is stripped first but the two that name the account
    (lessons/claude-code.md: a child of a session saves no transcript) — dispatch's screen
    runner strips them too, its headless fallback does not;
  - it waits for dispatch to return, which is the watcher's end.
After the end, in `finally`, whatever happened before:
  - the lock is released if it still names this session, and a `finishing` marker removed (R29:
    a `-p` exit once left owner.json naming a dead session; the watcher frees it too, this does
    not rely on that). A waiter ended while its session still runs ends that session first:
    a lock and a marker freed under a live /finish would let the checkpoint hook commit into its
    rewrite;
  - the outcome, read from the transcript's last reply, never from a return code (`launched` was
    `landed` before rename S4; every reader takes both, common.LAUNCHED_OUTCOMES):
      launched       a DONE closing line (common.ending), `Shipped:` or a `rollback:` line, and no
                     ⚠ anywhere → pr N from `merged #N`, the PR link, the Shipped line, or GitHub
      stopped        any ⚠ line, whatever else the reply says (a stop may well name `merged #57`
                     or a rollback: review, 2026-10-05); gate `G<n>` from the first ⚠ line
                     (`⚠ step 4 — …`, as the skill's --unattended paragraph asks), else from its
                     words, else `stop`; no ⚠ and no launch: `nothing-to-land` or `no-landing`
                     (an ending /finish never writes, R16)
      never-started  no reply in the transcript: gate `never-started` and the screen's last lines
                     (the watcher's hardcopy), or `refused` when dispatch refused to start;
  - autofinish.json updated (`ended`, `outcome`, `pr`, `gate`); a stop posted as one comment on the
    branch's PR through hooks/common.py's api; a line in `<common git dir>/claude/autofinished`
    (`launched <branch> #N` — no number when none was read —, `stopped <branch> <gate> <line>` or
    `never-started <branch> <line>`: the shapes bin/autofinish's header gives, design section 7);
    and one line in ~/Library/Logs/claude-weekly/autofinish-<date>.log,
    `<iso> run <repo> <branch> <outcome> …`.

A runner that dies — the Mac lost power, the process was killed — never reaches that `finally`
(2026-10-06: two rule-D runs, a laptop at 1 %). bin/autofinish's sweep asks `dead()` about every
worktree whose state says started with no outcome, and `bury()` writes what the `finally` would
have, as outcome `died` (docs/autofinish-died-2026-10-07.md):
  - dead: the waiter itself is gone (its pid and start time in autofinish-waiter.json — while it
    runs, nothing else is asked: a Mac asleep before dispatch wrote its record is not a death);
    then neither its claude (the pid with its recorded start time: a reused pid is not it) nor
    dispatch's watcher runs, and dispatch wrote no end; or dispatch ended it PRE_START_S ago and
    no outcome followed (the waiter died); or no dispatch record PRE_START_S after the start.
    A run dispatch ended is recorded with the outcome its transcript gives (record(), as the
    waiter would have) — a launch or a stop is not a death;
  - autofinish.json ended `died` (gate null), the lock released and a `finishing` marker removed
    when the lock still names the dead session (or none does), one comment on the PR, a line
    `<iso> died <branch> <tip7> <dispatch log's last line>` in `autofinished` and
    `<iso> run <repo> <branch> died session=… tip=… rule=… by=… account=… — <that line>` in the day's log.
    The override marker stays: the branch is judged again, and a rule-C branch needs it for that.
  A died run's tip is no "same tip" refusal here: bin/autofinish retries it once (its header).

The sessions it works on are told (the owner, 2026-10-18: "message every open/active session it works
on … and provide details of the session that's running it"). At the start, before the lock changes
hands, the live sessions open on the worktree — bin/autofinish's sessions_on(): the lock's holder,
the last prompter, any whose cwd is inside it; a typed hand-off's caller left out, it handed the
branch over — go into `<git dir>/claude/autofinish-notify.json` ({"session", "targets", "ts",
"told"}). A write that fails is said and skipped, never a failed start. SendMessage is a model tool
and no CLI verb sends one (2.1.302), so the run's own session sends them, as its first act under
`/finish --unattended` (skills/finish), each send judged by the classifier — no allow rule:

    autofinish-run notify [WORKTREE]

prints, per target still live, `SendMessage to <name>` (with its cwd where another live session
shares the name) and the text: the branch is taken, and the run's session id, how ListAgents lists
it (by its name, finish-<topic> fitted to the core's session_name_max, and by its cwd too), its
Remote Control name as dispatch recorded it, account, rule, start, tip, record and log. A target with no inbox, or gone since, is
named and skipped: checkpoint.py's once-notice at its next prompt carries the run's session,
account and rule. So does a send across logins that SendMessage refuses — unproven either way
(docs/session-asks-2026-10-09.md, B0c), which is why the day's log line says `asked=`, not sent.
Only the run's own session gets the lines (CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID; unset is refused), only while
the run is under way, and once (`told`). Exit 0, also with nothing to send.

The finisher rotation (S2 of docs/bench-continuous-2026-10-24.md; the owner's builders D1: A,
2026-10-24): while the config key `wharf-runs/finisher-rotation` says `until YYYY-MM-DD` and that day
has not passed, each start draws its finisher by a fair coin — ARMS, today's finisher (Opus 5.5 at
`medium`: dispatch's opus and the skill's `effort: medium`) or Sonnet 5.5 at `medium` — and passes the
arm's full model id and effort to dispatch, both arms alike. The key is the one switch and its end: no
key, a passed day or a line that does not parse is today's start, unchanged. The fence comes first:
a repo in any scope that is `confidential: true`, a repo in no scope, or a registry that does not
read or walk (read_registry) keeps today's finisher and writes no row anywhere.
The drawn arm goes into autofinish.json as `arm` ({name, model, effort, coin, until, forced}), and at
the end — record(), or bury() for a death — one JSON row goes to <logs>/finisher-rotation.jsonl,
outside git, private to this Mac: host, repo, branch, session, the coin and arm, the models the
transcript returned, the outcome, gate and PR, and the cost of the session and of its agents, priced
by plumb's usage-report. The fence is asked again there, so a repo that turned confidential meanwhile
writes nothing. AUTOFINISH_COIN=0|1 forces the coin (the tests), and the row says `forced`; `rotation`
counts forced rows apart, never in an arm. A row whose session returned no model at all is `voided:
no-reply`, one that never answered in its arm's model `voided: fallback`. With the key file there but
the rotation off or fenced, the start line says `arm=off` and why; with no key file it is unchanged.
Since task-shape S1 (docs/task-shape-2026-10-29.md) every finish run writes its row, the rotation on or off:
`arm`, `coin` null when none was drawn, `model` then dispatch's record of the run and `effort` the finish skill's
(`medium`, today's arm), and `rotation`
counts those rows apart (`(no arm)`), never in an arm; the fence is the draw's, so a fenced repo still writes
none. The record (autofinish.json) and the row gain `base` (main_of), `base_tip` (origin's base at the run's
end, by ls-remote) and `pushed`: the sha the run's own last `git push` of the branch sent, read from that push's
report in the transcript and kept only when origin's branch holds it at the run's end; a later push, a branch
origin no longer has or no push read leaves it out (unknown), and the local HEAD is never it alone: a push
of the branch that found origin "Everything up-to-date" (the checkpoint hook pushed first) keeps origin's head
only while the worktree's HEAD is that head too. `ended` is stamped before the ls-remote, at the run's end.

    autofinish-run rotation [--json] [--settle] [--since YYYY-MM-DD]

reads the rows back: per arm, the launches, stops by gate, deaths and median costs, and each host's
share of the runs. --settle reads, for each launched row 48 h old with no answer yet, its PR's files
and merge time from GitHub, and the commits on the repo's origin/main within the next 48 h that touch
those files (the PR's own subjects left out): `touched_48h`, and `fix_48h` for the ones whose subject
says fix, revert, regression, broke or bug; then writes them into the row. A `no-landing` row (a finish
that readied its PR for automerge) is settled first (settle_landing): `landing: settled`, read as `launched
(settled)` and then given its 48 h like a launch, only when its PR merged after the run started, into the
recorded base, at the head the run pushed, that head not the base's tip, with a merge commit apart from it,
someone recorded as merging it, a diff that is not empty, and no later finish run on the branch started before
the merge (a row here of the same repo by its real path, the run's worktree state, or a line in the repo's
`autofinished` dated after the row: a peer's run writes its row on that Mac); once the PR is closed and one fails, `landing: unknown` and `landing_why` names which; a row missing a
field (every row from before S1) is never read. A row of a repo that is
confidential now is passed over, and an unreadable registry refuses the verb, exit 3.

The run's own output (dispatch's log and this child's) goes to
~/Library/Logs/claude-weekly/<date>-dispatch-finish-<topic>.log. --dry prints what would be
written and run — the state, the lock, the bundle's head, and dispatch's own dry run, claude's
whole command line included — and writes nothing.

What it prints is dispatch's verdict, read from the run's log after the detach and never predicted (night-landings
finding 1: two `started:` lines for runs dispatch then refused for room): `started: …` once the header
(`== claude <pid>, session <this run's sid>, …`) is in the log; `not started: <dispatch's FAILED text>` when it
refused and the waiter has recorded the end (never-started at `refused`, the lock released); and, with no verdict
in VERDICT_S (120 s, less what the caller's own wait leaves), `not confirmed after N s: …` on stdout, exit 0 — the
run may still begin, so every caller keeps reading it as the start it read before this verdict existed (cold_prompt
blocks the typed /finish, dispatch leaves the slot to the landing); the waiter goes on and settles it, nothing is
started twice. The log is polled, dispatch never run again; it is per day and topic, not per repo, so only a header
naming this run's session counts, and a FAILED line only once this session's state has ended (a sibling repo's
run of the same topic writes the same log). With a queue home the verdict is the put's (Where): `queued: …` on
stdout, exit 0. `not started` goes to stderr, which a sweep quotes.
Exit: 0 started or queued (or --dry), 1 the detach failed (nothing ran, the lock given back), 2 usage or not
a claude/* worktree, 3 refused (same tip, a run going, another start under way, a lock in use, no
account may run it now), 6 the origin gate refused (origin's branch holds what this tip lacks: nothing ran; cold_prompt
blocks on it, where any other refusal lets a typed /finish run in its tab), 4 the ship's lease refuses the caller's generation (DISPATCH_SHIP; S4),
5 dispatch refused the start (no room, the cap, memory, no account: nothing ran, the tip may start again); no
verdict in time is 0, said `not confirmed`.
Tests (bin/test_autofinish_run.sh) replace dispatch's commands with DISPATCH_CLAUDE, _SCREEN and the
rest; AUTOFINISH_HOST names this host, AUTOFINISH_QUEUE dispatch-queue, AUTOFINISH_END_WARM `dispatch-rc end-warm`,
AUTOFINISH_SEND_BUDGET its constant, AUTOFINISH_DAY (YYYY-MM-DD) the day Tree.runlog and log() take (log_day();
runlog_of keeps the run's start day, and bin/autofinish's sweep lines the real clock), so a case run across local
midnight reads the file it wrote (the night-landings digest's finding 7: a CI run at 00:00, 2026-10-26); AUTOFINISH_VERDICT_S and _POLL the verdict wait's bound and poll, AUTOFINISH_DISPATCH replaces dispatch itself, AUTOFINISH_ACCOUNT bin/account (dispatch's
DISPATCH_ACCOUNT; the tests mostly run the real one on a fixture registry), AUTOFINISH_API_STUB names a Python file whose
`api()` stands in for common.api. Python 3.9, stdlib only.
"""
